<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  WHERE PACKAGES COME FROM IS A PROPERTY OF THIS CHECKOUT, NOT OF THE MACHINE BUILDING IT.

  Without this file NuGet composes its configuration from the machine's own: %APPDATA%\NuGet\
  NuGet.Config and every .config under "C:\Program Files (x86)\NuGet\Config". Anything installed
  there joins the restore silently, and nothing in the repository says so.

  That is not hypothetical here. Installing Syncfusion Essential Studio writes
  "Syncfusion Toolbox for WPF.config" into that machine-level directory, and it registers the
  installer's own package folder as a fallback:

      C:\Program Files (x86)\Syncfusion\Essential Studio\WPF\34.1.29\ToolboxNuGetPackages

  A fallback folder is consulted for an exact id and version, so today it supplies nothing: it
  holds the 34.1.29 meta-package and this solution pins the individual packages at 34.1.32. The
  hazard is what it would supply if those ever lined up. THE ASSEMBLIES IN THAT FOLDER COME FROM
  THE LICENSED INSTALLER AND ARE PRE-LICENSED, so a build resolving from it shows no Syncfusion
  trial banner whether or not a key was registered. A developer with Essential Studio installed
  would see a clean shell, CI would not, and the difference would be invisible in the diff. That
  is the whole reason this file exists; see DEPENDENCIES.md for the licensing model itself.

  So: clear what is inherited and name what is used.

    packageSources        cleared, then nuget.org alone. This is the one clause that could have
                          broken the build rather than merely tightened it: the machine-level
                          Visual Studio offline source had supplied three packages here
                          (System.Reflection.Emit, System.Reflection.Metadata and
                          System.Runtime.InteropServices.RuntimeInformation), so removing it is
                          only safe if nuget.org serves them too. Measured rather than assumed, by
                          restoring the whole solution into an EMPTY packages folder with this file
                          in place: 80 package versions downloaded, every one of them recording
                          nuget.org as its source, and no other source consulted.

    disabledPackageSources
                          cleared as well, and not for tidiness. A machine config can DISABLE a
                          source by name, and clearing packageSources does not undo that. A machine
                          that had disabled "nuget.org" would therefore disable the one source we
                          re-add, and the restore would fail for a reason no file in the tree
                          mentions.

    fallbackPackageFolders
                          cleared. This is the Syncfusion entry above, and the Visual Studio
                          shared folder alongside it. Nothing the solution restores was coming
                          from either: all 68 package libraries named across the projects' asset
                          files were already present in the global cache, none of them only in a
                          fallback folder.

  The global packages folder (%USERPROFILE%\.nuget\packages) is deliberately NOT pinned. It is a
  cache, not a source; overriding it would make every developer re-download the world for no gain.
-->
<configuration>

  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <disabledPackageSources>
    <clear />
  </disabledPackageSources>

  <fallbackPackageFolders>
    <clear />
  </fallbackPackageFolders>

</configuration>
